<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experiments</h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Experiment Setups</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Baselines</span>. We benchmarked our approach against a suite of representative baseline methods, encompassing both <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">training-based approaches</span>, such as ESD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>, RECE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>, TRCE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>, and <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">training-free approaches</span>, such as SLD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite>, Safree <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>]</cite>, and AdaVD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Evaluation Metrics</span>.
We systematically evaluate the efficacy of our concept erasing method across two distinct domains: (1) broad concepts, (2) narrow concepts, including styles and Intellectual Property(IP). Our evaluation protocol is designed to measure both erasure efficacy and utility preservation.
For the broad concept task, we employ the Q16 detector <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite> and the NudeNet detector <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite> to calculate the rate of images flagged as ’inappropriate’.
For narrow concepts, we referred to the previous method <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>, <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite> and evaluated our approach using <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>50</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">50</annotation></semantics></math> IP templates and <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="30" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>30</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">30</annotation></semantics></math> art style templates, generating 10 images for each template.
To assess image generation quality and prior preservation, we employ the CLIP score, FID, LPIPS and Aesthetic score as evaluation metrics. For the IP task, we similarly utilize CLIP score and FID as the primary metrics.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Training Setups / Implementation Details</span>. We use SD v1.4 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite> as the base model. To assess performance on broad concept tasks, we primarily utilize four benchmarks: the I2P dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite> comprising 4,703 user prompts, and three additional sets of adversarial prompts generated through red-teaming frameworks: Ring-a-Bell <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite>, Prompt4Debugging <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>, and UnlearnDiff <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>]</cite>.
For all the experiments, we use DDIM scheduler sampling for <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="30" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>30</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">30</annotation></semantics></math> steps to generate images, and set guidance scale to <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>7.5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7.5</annotation></semantics></math> as a normal CFG config for most of the experiments.
In the data preparation stage, we employed specific prompt templates to generate prompts involving the target concepts as well as their near miss counterparts. For the malicious concept task, we generated <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="400" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>400</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">400</annotation></semantics></math> prompts per concept, while for the artistic style and IP tasks, we generated <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>100</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100</annotation></semantics></math> prompts per concept. For each prompt and its corresponding near miss pair, we generated <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> images, holding the seed constant for each pair. This ensures that the resulting images exhibit maximal similarity, differing primarily with respect to the target concept.
During training, the textual concept prototypes are optimized for <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2,000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2,000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2,000</annotation></semantics></math> iterations with a learning rate of <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5e-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5e-2</annotation></semantics></math>.
We implement <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-means clustering for generating concept prototypes, and adjust the number of prototypes by setting different <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> values according to the breadth of a target concept.
Specifically, we set the number of prototypes to <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>16</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">16</annotation></semantics></math> for the malicious concept task, <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> for style concepts, and <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> for IP concepts.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Overall Performance</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Broad Concept Erasure</span>.
We first evaluate our approach on the broad-concept erasure task using the seven safety-critical categories from the I2P dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite>: <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">hate</span>, <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">harassment</span>, <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">illegal activity</span>, <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">self-harm</span>, <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">sexual</span>, <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">shocking</span>, and <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">violence</span>.
In this experiment, we directly employ a multi-concept erasure setup by aggregating all prototypes into a single prototype bank, as prompts in I2P may invoke multiple harmful categories simultaneously.
Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3.3 Concept Erasure with Prototype Guidance ‣ 3 Prototype-Guided Concept Erasure ‣ Prototype-Guided Concept Erasure in Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> reports the proportion of generated images flagged as <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">inappropriate</span> by the Q16 detector <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite>.
Our method consistently achieves the lowest or near-lowest detection rate across all categories, resulting in the best overall performance.
Notably, our approach performs consistently well on semantically diverse concepts such as <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">violence</span>, <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">sexual</span>, and <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">harassment</span>, where prior methods often show large variations across sub-categories.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We also conducted experiments on adversarial attacks. As shown in Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4.2 Overall Performance ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Prototype-Guided Concept Erasure in Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, we use Attack Success Rate (ASR) as the metric to measure the erasure effect, and FID as the metric to measure the knowledge preserving capability. It is worth mentioning that our method was not designed for adversarial attacks, but still achieved good results in most cases.
In order to evaluate the generalization performance, we referred to Safree <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>]</cite> and conducted experiments on SDXL and SD3.5 as extended models. Results in Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 4.2 Overall Performance ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Prototype-Guided Concept Erasure in Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> demonstrate that our method also has excellent performance on other models and possesses strong compatibility.</p>
</div>
